        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(gateway): tighten CSP connect-src from bare ws:/wss: to explicit endpoints",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe Control UI CSP `connect-src` directive used bare scheme sources `ws:` and `wss:`. Per CSP spec, a bare scheme source allows connections to **any** host — meaning any XSS in the Control UI could exfiltrate data via WebSocket to an arbitrary server.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nControl UI actually only needs three specific WebSocket/connection targets beyond `'self'`:\n1. **Gateway WebSocket** — same origin, covered by `'self'`\n2. **OpenAI API** — `https://api.openai.com` (already explicitly listed, HTTPS)\n3. **Google Gemini Live** — `wss://generativelanguage.googleapis.com` for realtime voice feature (was relying on bare `wss:`)\n\nRather than allowing \"any WebSocket anywhere\", the policy now explicitly names each endpoint.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- **No visible change** — all existing functionality works identically\n- Defense-in-depth improvement if a future XSS vulnerability is discovered in the Control UI\n\n## Evidence\n\n- ~60 CSP unit tests pass (4 test files, all green)~\n- Verified that:\n  - `gateway.ts:520` — Gateway WebSocket uses page origin (`'self'`)\n  - `realtime-talk-google-live.ts:106` — Google Live WebSocket uses `wss://generativelanguage.googleapis.com` (now explicitly listed)\n  - `realtime-talk-webrtc.ts:126` — OpenAI Realtime uses HTTPS `fetch` to `api.openai.com` (already listed)\n",
        "branch": "fix/csp-connect-src-loopback-only",
